.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/get-nonce Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "get-nonce"
.PP
just returns a \fBnonce\fP (number used once)\&. No batteries included in those 46 bytes of this library\&.

.PP
.PP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
✅ build in \fRwebpack\fP support via \fR__webpack_nonce__\fP
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRgetNonce(): string|undefined\fP - returns the current \fRnonce\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsetNonce(newValue)\fP - set's nonce value
.PP
.SS "Why?"
Why we need a library to access \fR__webpack_nonce__\fP? Abstractions!

.PP
"I", as a library author, don't want to "predict" the platform "you" going to use\&. "I", as well, want an easier way to test and control \fRnonce\fP value\&.

.PP
Like - \fRnonce\fP is supported out of the box only by webpack, what you are going to do?

.PP
This is why this "man-in-the-middle" was created\&. Yep, think about \fRleft-pad\fP :)
.SS "Webpack"
.RS 4
https://webpack.js.org/guides/csp/ 
.RE
.PP

.PP
To activate the feature set a \fBwebpack_nonce\fP variable needs to be included in your entry script\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
__webpack_nonce__ = uuid(); // for example
.fi
.PP

.PP
Without \fRwebpack\fP \fR__webpack_nonce__\fP is actually just a global variable, which makes it actually bundler independent, however "other bundlers" are able to replicate it only setting it as a global variable (as here in tests) which violates a "secure" nature of \fRnonce\fP\&.

.PP
\fRget-nonce\fP is not global\&.
.SS "Used in"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreact-style-singleton\fP <- \fRreact-remove-scroll\fP <- \fRreact-focus-on\fP
.PP
